WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:09.583
In some cultures, sleep is considered a time when the soul is able to leave the body and

2
00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:14.245
traverse a spiritual universe, encountering long-dead friends and mystical creatures.

3
00:00:15.086 --> 00:00:17.987
To these people, it is an important component of life.

4
00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:24.990
In other cultures, however, sleep is regarded as a waste of time, an unwelcome necessity that prevents people

5
00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.670
from working 24 hours a day.

6
00:00:27.471 --> 00:00:33.413
Students cramming for exams often drink lots of coffee, which contains the stimulant caffeine, to keep awake.

7
00:00:34.153 --> 00:00:39.395
The American military is even researching a drug that will keep combat soldiers awake so they can do

8
00:00:39.475 --> 00:00:43.056
two forty-hour shifts of duty separated by eight hours of sleep.

9
00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:45.797
Work will not wait for the dreaming, they say.

10
00:00:46.517 --> 00:00:50.878
But no matter how hard people try to stay awake, eventually, sleep will prevail.

11
00:00:51.658 --> 00:00:56.400
After all, it is a fundamental part of the human experience─and it is inescapable.

12
00:00:57.672 --> 00:01:03.998
Scientists did not begin studying the phenomenon of sleep in earnest until the mid-twentieth century, when an American

13
00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:11.444
biologist named Franz Halberg noticed that animals seem to regulate their sleeping, eating, and waking behavior in a

14
00:01:11.484 --> 00:01:13.005
natural 24-hour pattern.

15
00:01:13.826 --> 00:01:16.308
Halberg called this pattern the "circadian rhythm."

16
00:01:16.949 --> 00:01:22.333
Through a series of studies on humans and other animals, Halberg and his team of experts determined that

17
00:01:22.393 --> 00:01:29.317
this cycle is both psychological and physiological; brainwave activity changes and the level of hormones in the body

18
00:01:29.357 --> 00:01:31.978
surges and declines, based on the time of day.

19
00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:38.580
This pattern works like a daily schedule for the body, telling it when to perform certain tasks─one reason

20
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.780
it is also known as the biological clock.

21
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:47.262
In addition, Halberg found that exposure to sunlight is an external trigger that can prompt animals and humans

22
00:01:47.302 --> 00:01:51.203
to wake up and to look for food, while darkness tends to bring on sleep.

23
00:01:52.463 --> 00:01:57.830
While Halberg was running his studies, other scientists were beginning to research what happens in the body during

24
00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:05.673
sleep. They discovered that sleep can be clearly divided into two different types: REM (rapid eye movement) sleep

25
00:02:06.213 --> 00:02:07.594
and non-REM sleep.

26
00:02:08.414 --> 00:02:15.197
Throughout REM sleep, which often involves strange and random dreams, the eyes move briskly under closed lids, as

27
00:02:15.277 --> 00:02:17.178
if they are frantically reading a newspaper.

28
00:02:18.078 --> 00:02:23.593
Scientists believe that adults experience four or five periods of REM sleep during a normal night, about once

29
00:02:23.653 --> 00:02:25.374
every ninety minutes to two hours.

30
00:02:26.255 --> 00:02:32.741
Doctors have noticed that patients in REM mode show brainwave activity, irregular breathing, and heart rates similar to

31
00:02:32.761 --> 00:02:33.982
those of people who are awake.

32
00:02:34.742 --> 00:02:42.009
However, their motor neurons, cells that stimulate muscle movement, are dormant; therefore, the body remains completely still.

33
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:49.355
In contrast to REM sleep, non-REM sleep is when our brains are inactive but our bodies are noticeably

34
00:02:49.476 --> 00:02:54.523
active. Non-REM sleep accounts for more than three-quarters of a human's total sleep time.

35
00:02:55.323 --> 00:03:01.067
It happens in four stages, with the first, known as "drowsy rest," occurring at the onset of sleep.

36
00:03:01.868 --> 00:03:04.810
People may twitch or their limbs may jerk involuntarily.

37
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.214
Their brains stop producing "alpha waves," brainwaves that generally indicate active brain function.

38
00:03:10.974 --> 00:03:16.718
Then, as they lose awareness of their surroundings, they enter the second and longest stage, which can occupy

39
00:03:16.778 --> 00:03:18.980
more than half of all non-REM sleep time.

40
00:03:19.656 --> 00:03:25.339
Stages three and four, which represent the deepest periods of sleep, are when people might experience night terrors

41
00:03:25.359 --> 00:03:32.343
(episodes in which people experience intense fear, but that are usually not remembered in the morning), sleepwalking, and

42
00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:37.726
bed-wetting. It is extremely difficult to wake someone who is in stage four of non-REM sleep.

43
00:03:39.087 --> 00:03:44.069
The function of the different stages of sleep is not well understood, but several theories for the role

44
00:03:44.089 --> 00:03:45.750
of REM sleep have been proposed.

45
00:03:46.551 --> 00:03:53.028
One suggests that during this time, the brain consolidates memories, reinforcing information that it has learned during the

46
00:03:53.048 --> 00:03:56.251
day and incorporating it into long-term memory storage.

47
00:03:56.891 --> 00:03:58.973
This allows it to be retrieved at a later point.

48
00:03:59.794 --> 00:04:04.979
Another theory is that REM sleep is part of a recovery system that shuts down sensitive brain receptors

49
00:04:05.359 --> 00:04:10.343
that are constantly being damaged throughout the day and gives them time to rebuild and become healthy again.

50
00:04:11.605 --> 00:04:18.294
Alternatively, the ontogenetic hypothesis of REM sleep, which is based on the theory of how organisms develop from

51
00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:24.559
their earliest stages into adults, proposes that REM sleep plays an essential role in the development of the

52
00:04:24.659 --> 00:04:25.300
infant brain.

53
00:04:26.221 --> 00:04:33.046
According to this theory, REM sleep provides the neural stimulation that helps infants develop mature brains and nervous

54
00:04:33.066 --> 00:04:37.770
systems. Newborn babies experience longer periods of REM sleep than adults do.

55
00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:44.135
According to this theory, babies need more REM sleep because they receive an enormous amount of new information

56
00:04:44.375 --> 00:04:49.530
during waking hours compared with their parents, who already have a good understanding of how the world works.

57
00:04:50.391 --> 00:04:55.734
Some scientists also believe that if infants are regularly not allowed to sleep through the night, they will

58
00:04:55.774 --> 00:04:57.875
fail to develop a healthy brain later in life.

59
00:04:59.216 --> 00:05:02.898
However, what constitutes a good night of sleep is debatable.

60
00:05:03.958 --> 00:05:07.600
Most Western doctors think that adults should get at least eight hours each night.

61
00:05:08.281 --> 00:05:13.764
But many people have trouble staying asleep for a full night, and this has encouraged one researcher, Carol

62
00:05:14.444 --> 00:05:18.628
Worthman, to consider whether or not it is possible to sleep less and still remain functional.

63
00:05:19.368 --> 00:05:24.532
To explore this question, she visited several different communities in Africa, Europe, and Asia.

64
00:05:25.113 --> 00:05:26.514
What she discovered was startling.

65
00:05:27.375 --> 00:05:33.160
It seems that in many societies, instead of sleeping for an uninterrupted eight hours, people break up their

66
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.781
sleeping time into two pieces.

67
00:05:35.742 --> 00:05:41.126
Members of one African tribe, for instance, go to bed at dusk for four hours (the first sleep),

68
00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:46.981
wake up for several minutes─during which time they check the safety of their children and feed the fire─and

69
00:05:47.021 --> 00:05:53.543
then fall back asleep for another four hours (the second sleep), eventually reviving around sunrise.

70
00:05:54.463 --> 00:05:59.785
In the Mediterranean, many people take short naps in the mid-afernoon when the temperature is too hot to

71
00:05:59.845 --> 00:06:03.045
work, and then sleep only four to six hours at night.

72
00:06:03.786 --> 00:06:07.607
Yet, both Africans and Mediterraneans feel well rested in the morning.

73
00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:13.949
There are reports of this kind of sleeping pattern in English-language literature written before industrialization.

74
00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:21.075
Worthman believes that as people began to work in factories with bright artificial lighting, their sleeping patterns shifted.

75
00:06:21.896 --> 00:06:28.181
Furthermore, she thinks that this transition from a two-part sleep to one long sleep is partially responsible for

76
00:06:28.201 --> 00:06:30.763
the increasing number of sleep disorders in Western society.

77
00:06:31.463 --> 00:06:37.148
Perhaps insomniacs are merely ignoring their circadian rhythms and trying to force themselves to sleep when they are

78
00:06:37.188 --> 00:06:37.768
not tired.

79
00:06:38.469 --> 00:06:43.345
People who are frustrated because they always wake up in the middle of the night may be experiencing

80
00:06:43.605 --> 00:06:45.926
the natural break between first and second sleeps.

81
00:06:47.207 --> 00:06:51.969
But however we sleep, it is a fact that all humans spend approximately a third of their lives

82
00:06:52.029 --> 00:06:56.812
slumbering. Some will nestle in comfortable beds, resting their heads on fluffy pillows.

83
00:06:57.592 --> 00:06:59.934
Others might stretch out on a blanket under the stars.

84
00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:05.577
Still others will lie down on thick cotton mattresses on the floor, covered with light quilts.

85
00:07:06.297 --> 00:07:10.159
Hopefully, all of them will get the rest their bodies crave before they have to get up and

86
00:07:10.199 --> 00:07:11.340
get back to waking life.
